SUMMARY:To Die a Frenchman
DESCRIPTION:80 min | Tamil with English Subtitles | 2026 | India \nMonsieur Mounissamy is a seventy-year-old man living in the crumbling neighbourhood of Ville Noir in Pondicherry\, where French street names fade into dust and memories of empire linger. Each July 14th\, French National Day\, he unfurls a flag mirroring the French tricolour and sings\, in trembling\, imperfect French\, an anthem to a “Mother France” he believes could still reclaim him. He spends his life fighting for French citizenship and a tiny plot of Indian land\, anchoring himself in histories that abandoned him. What do we call those who never crossed a border\, yet woke up one day to find themselves citizens of a country they did not choose? \nWhen colonial France withdrew from Pondicherry without a plebiscite\, it disregarded its own constitutional principles\, and native Tamils became unintended bystanders in a political contest between two nations. Mounissamy’s longing for French citizenship was not merely a legal pursuit\, but an attempt to reclaim an identity stolen from him. Shot over five years\, this cinematic elegy looks beyond Pondicherry’s romanticised French facade to confront colonialism\, citizenship and belonging. \nThe screening will be followed by a discussion between Pankaj Rishi Kumar\, the director and Ramjee Chandran. The session will end with an audience Q&A. \nThis event is part of a double bill of films exploring French colonial histories\, memories and their lasting legacies. We invite you to join us for both screenings. View the accompanying film here. \nIn collaboration with: \n \nVenue: Seminar Halls\n\n\nWhen French Colonial Histories Become Part of Everyday Life\nFor Ages 14+\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfhiJIZw_ZGmxCllE4vHibipKofmUnaEPB88AKxztyS-7Px8g/viewform?usp=publish-editor)\n*Speakers*: Pankaj Rishi Kumar\, Ramjee Chandran\n*Event Categories*: Film\, History\, In Memory
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/to-die-a-frenchman/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Film,History,In Memory
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Thumbnail_TDAF.jpg
END:VEVENT
