SUMMARY:Départ
DESCRIPTION:54 mins | India | 2026 | French\, English\, and Hindi\, with English Subtitles \nMahé is a small\, former French colony on the Malabar Coast of India. It carries a layered history shaped by colonial encounters\, migration\, language\, memory\, and cultural exchange. The film asks what remains of the French presence in Mahé today\, through honest conversations\, surreal landscapes and lived experiences. Why do colonial histories continue to inhabit everyday life long after political rule has ended? \nDépart is an attempt to understand what remains when an entire chapter of history has passed\, and what is quietly lost when the last voices capable of telling that story fall silent. The film invites audiences to look beyond the familiar narratives of colonialism\, toward the quieter\, more intimate ways history survives in places\, communities\, identities\, and memories. \nThis event is part of a double bill of films exploring French colonial histories\, memories and their lasting legacies. We invite you to join us for both screenings. View the accompanying film here. \nIn collaboration with:\n \nVenue: Seminar Halls\n\n\nWhen French Colonial Histories Become Part of Everyday Life\nFor Ages 13+\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdOZolHPBzs1rQvfXDK1qfWzgBvsaMwrh8qtG9yUrpVZaxXwQ/viewform?usp=pp_url)\n*Speaker*: Mrinalini Tewari\n*Event Categories*: Culture\, Film
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/depart/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Culture,Film
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Thumbnail_Depart.jpg
